From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:34:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: WhatsApp voice and video calls through the Phone app

* WhatsApp voice and video calls through the Phone app

Attach meowcaller (github.com/purpshell/meowcaller, a pure-Go WhatsApp
VoIP stack) to the plugin's own whatsmeow client, so the account that
is paired for messaging can also place and receive calls, and expose it
on the bus as the luna-service2 service com.palm.whatsapp.call, which
the stock webOS Phone app drives.

- call.go: the Go side - dial/answer/hang up, call state, audio frames
  between meowcaller and C, video access units in and out
- glue/call.c: the LS2 service (callStateQuery etc.), ALSA capture and
  playback, and a video test-injection harness (glue/test_stream_data.h
  holds a recorded H.264 stream for it)
- call audio plays on the ALSA "voip" device and is captured from
  "voipsource", the PulseAudio call sink and source; the call is
  reported to audiod with phone/CallStatusUpdate (transport
  com.palm.whatsapp), and audiod picks the output device itself, the
  earpiece first. The service registers a single LS2 handle: LuneOS
  has one bus, so the public/private split of legacy webOS is not used
- glue/denoise.c, glue/aec.cc: WebRTC noise suppression (compiled out,
  see denoise.c) and the mobile echo canceller (AECM) for the
  microphone path; the echo canceller is webrtc-audio-processing-1's
  AudioProcessing in mobile mode, linked from the system library
- glue/h264_rtp.c, glue/voipkit*: the video bridge. voipkit.cpp binds
  libpalmgstskype.so, which exists only in legacy webOS firmware;
  voipkit_none.c is the backend used where it is absent (LuneOS)
- the service is registered when the plugin loads rather than at
  account login, so a dial request never reaches a missing service
- an incoming call is shown as an "Incoming call" message that turns
  into "Missed call" if it is not answered
- the client presents itself as an Android phone (platform type), since
  peers were seen not to start video decoding for an UNKNOWN platform
- whatsmeow's debug lines that carry call signalling are logged at
  info level

meowcaller is not carried here. go.mod requires it at
v0.0.0-20260906234207-c48c3e2a243c and replaces it with
./third_party/meowcaller, where the build places a checkout of that
commit with the webOS patches applied (encoder speed-ups for ARMv7,
video path fixes, multi-device calls and relay election).

meowcaller leaves a peer's request to turn its video on to the
application (Call.AcceptVideo), so OnVideoState accepts it when
VideoState.Upgrade is set; our own camera stays as it is.

* Ring an incoming WhatsApp call without taking the call audio

An incoming call opened the ALSA voip streams and reported an active
call to audiod as soon as the offer arrived, when meowcaller sends the
preaccept, so audiod paused the music and switched to the call route
while the phone was still ringing. A call nobody answered or one that
was rejected went through the same, and closing the audio when it
ended also closed it under any other call still in progress.

The call audio path is now taken by an outgoing call when it is
placed, as before, and by an incoming call only when it is answered,
just before Answer starts the media so the microphone is already
capturing. callaudio.Path counts the calls that hold the path: it is
opened by the first and closed with the last, and ending a call that
never held it, such as a rejected or missed one, leaves it alone. If
Answer fails, the call lets go of the path again.

callaudio is plain Go without cgo, so its tests run on their own:
  go test ./callaudio

* Keep the WhatsApp call playback stream fed between packets

Incoming call audio was written to the ALSA "voip" device only when a
decoded packet arrived. Packets do not arrive at a steady pace: the
relay delivers them in bursts and the peer sends nothing while it is
silent (DTX). Any gap longer than the 120 ms playback buffer ran the
pulse plug-in's stream dry. The next write then failed with -EPIPE and
snd_pcm_recover() prepared the PCM. The pulse plug-in prepares by
disconnecting its PulseAudio stream and connecting a new one, so every
gap meant a new sink-input for palm-policy to route and audiod to set
up. After that nothing played until the buffer had filled to its start
threshold again, and the frame whose write failed was lost.

On the mindphone this showed as the "ALSA plug-in [imlibpurpleservice]"
sink-input on voipcall being closed and reopened about once a second.
It was opened 19 times in one call and 31 times in the next. Replaying
the packet arrival times meowcaller recorded for those two calls through
a model of the old write path gives 18 and 28 underruns, against the 18
and 30 reopenings. The audio was choppy in the first call and missing
in the second.

The decoder now only queues its frames in a playout ring (glue/
playout.c). A playback thread opens the device once per call and writes
20 ms chunks from the ring at the device's pace, blocking in
snd_pcm_writei, with silence for whatever the ring cannot supply. The
stream therefore runs from the moment the call audio opens until it
closes. After the ring has run dry, the thread first collects 120 ms
again, or waits that long, before playing on, which absorbs the relay's
jitter. A burst beyond 360 ms drops its oldest samples, so a network
stall cannot leave the call delayed for its remaining length.

AECM's far-end reference is now fed from the playback thread with each
chunk once it has been written, silence included. The reference used to
be what had arrived, not what had been played, and stopped in every
gap.

An underrun can still happen if the thread is kept off the CPU for
longer than the buffer. It is then recovered as before, logged, and
counted in the "playback done" line printed when the call ends. That
line also gives the samples played, the silence written, the samples
dropped and the ring underflows.

The playback device is now owned by the thread that writes it. Before,
the speaker callback ran on a Go thread and wrote g_play, which the call
teardown could close at the same time. g_audio_on is atomic because it
is read on all of these threads.

The tests in glue/tests run playout.c on its own, and run call.c's
playback path against a fake ALSA device:
  make -C glue/tests check

* Decode WhatsApp video through luneos-rtc-engine

WhatsApp video calls had no picture. The video bridge was
voipkit_none, and the earlier session code in glue/call.c asks
palm://com.palm.mediad/service/clonk for a video session. That is a
legacy webOS mediad service, and no LuneOS recipe provides it.

The new backend, glue/voipkit_rtcengine.c, hands the access units to
luneos-rtc-engine (org.webosports.rtcengine) over the engine's
SOCK_SEQPACKET sockets, one Annex-B access unit per datagram:

- Receive: units from meowcaller go to the engine's rx socket, which
  decodes them into a gst-shm socket that the Phone app renders. Sends
  never block the media loop. A full socket drops the unit, holds the
  stream back until the next IDR and asks the peer for one (at most
  once a second). The bridge also holds back until an IDR when it
  first connects or reconnects after the engine went away.
- Send: a thread reads the engine's encoded camera units from its tx
  socket and passes them to meowcaller. Oversize units are dropped.

glue/call.c replaces the clonk block with an engine session:

- The session runs while the call carries video and the user accepted
  it (changeMedia incomingVideo). It asks for capabilities, then
  starts send+receive when the engine has a camera, receive-only
  otherwise.
- It falls back to receive-only when the engine has a camera but
  cannot encode ("tx start failed", e.g. no h264 encoder on sargo).
- It stops and retries once if the engine is left running from an
  earlier process.
- Answers that belong to a closed session are recognised by a
  generation counter, and whatever they brought up is stopped.
- While a session runs, the published callState carries
  videoURI/videoWidth/videoHeight for the Phone app.
- audiod gets the real call origin and incomingVideo/outgoingVideo,
  so its video policy keeps the call off the earpiece.
- Peer PLI/FIR become an engine requestKeyframe, rate-limited and
  only while we send.
- The TEST-ONLY injector (testInjectVideo) now drives the same
  session. It refuses to run during a call, and refuses a second run
  until testStopInjectVideo.

The prototypes of the functions Go calls are declared in glue/call.h.
call.go exports gowhatsapp_go_call_request_peer_keyframe() for the
bridge.

The recipe builds this backend instead of voipkit_none by setting
VOIPKIT_GLUE. The call service also needs rtc.operation and the engine
in its outbound list, which imlibpurpleservice provides.

Tests: glue/tests/call_video_test (20 tests: session life cycle,
fallbacks, stale answers, published state, audiod, injector) and
voipkit_rtcengine_test (12 tests: IDR gate, whole and ordered units,
full socket, reconnect, keyframe rate limit, send path), against a fake
LS2 bus and real socketpairs:
  make -C glue/tests check

* WhatsApp video calls on the phone: orientation, front camera, camera off, preview

Fixes from live video calls between LuneOS phones and Android on
2026-11-03.

- Orientation: the plugin tells the peer which way our picture is up
  (gowhatsapp_go_call_set_video_orientation) once the camera is sent,
  and passes the peer's orientation to the Phone app as
  incomingVideoOrientation. Only a state with the peer's camera on
  carries it: the upgrade handshake (states 4, 6, 11) says 0 and
  turned the picture.
- Camera: a video call opens the front camera where there are two.
  Camera off sends <video state=6> (StopVideo), as WhatsApp's own
  clients do.
- An OnVideoState for a call already gone from the table no longer
  closes video: its OnEnd does, and closing here shut the engine
  under a call that was still up.
- The engine decodes what it encodes into a 320x240 preview for the
  Phone app's self-view, turned like the sent picture and mirrored for
  the front camera.
- Sending at 1.2 Mbit/s (500 kbit/s looked heavily compressed at
  640x480) and passing peer keyframe requests to the encoder at most
  once a second.
- Messaging: a 1:1 call rings in the Phone app only; it no longer
  also posts "Incoming call" in the chat, which made Messaging alert a
  second time. An unanswered one is posted as "Missed call".

* A WhatsApp call answered on another device is not missed

An incoming call that the user took on another of their devices (the
phone, WhatsApp Web or Desktop) ended here with meowcaller's end reason
"accepted_elsewhere" passed on as the Phone app's cause, which it does
not know, and the chat got a "Missed call" bubble because the call never
went active here.

Tell the Phone app "answeredElsewhere", which it counts as answered (no
"Missed call" alert, filed as incoming), and post no missed call bubble
for such a terminate. Every other reason is passed on as before. The
reason is the token a caller sends the devices that did not take the
call (meowcaller signaling.TerminateReasonAcceptedElsewhere).

* Our camera again after audio only, and the phone's orientation

Turning our camera on again in a call that had gone back to audio only
now starts the engine session as well: the session is what encodes
our picture, and the wish to see video had been forgotten with audio
only, so the peer saw a frozen picture until it turned its own camera
on.

The orientation <video device_orientation> carries follows the phone.
The Phone app sends changeMedia {deviceOrientation} (the shell's
quarter turns: 0 top up, 1 left edge up, 2 upside down, 3 right edge
up) while our camera is sent; the peer is told the upright turn (1)
plus that for the front camera, minus it for the back one, at once. A
changeMedia with only the orientation changes no video direction.
Checked on sargo against Android, both ways sideways.

